   "source": [
    "# Phase 1 – Part B: Supervised Learning\n",
    "\n",
    "## XGBoost\n",
    "\n",
    "### 1. Model Selection Rationale\n",
    "XGBoost (Extreme Gradient Boosting) was selected because it is a powerful tree-based ensemble model. It builds decision trees sequentially, where each new tree learns to correct the errors made by the previous trees. This allows it to capture complex non-linear relationships and interactions between health indicators, and it typically performs very well on structured tabular data.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2. Data Preparation\n",
    "The preprocessed training and testing datasets were used. The target variable is `Diabetes_binary`, and the remaining 21 health indicators were used as input features. The unscaled version of the data was used because tree-based models split on feature thresholds, so they are not affected by feature scaling. The same train/test split as the other models was used to ensure a fair comparison."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "YxBt3iqFg0C2"
   },
   "execution_count": 2,
   "outputs": [],
