   "source": [
    "# Compare missed positives (FN) with correctly detected positives (TP)\n",
    "errors = X_test.copy()\n",
    "\n",
    "errors[\"y_true\"] = y_test.values\n",
    "errors[\"y_pred\"] = y_pred_tuned\n",
    "\n",
    "fn_cases = errors[\n",
    "    (errors[\"y_true\"] == 1) &\n",
    "    (errors[\"y_pred\"] == 0)\n",
    "]\n",
    "\n",
    "tp_cases = errors[\n",
    "    (errors[\"y_true\"] == 1) &\n",
    "    (errors[\"y_pred\"] == 1)\n",
    "]\n",
    "\n",
    "fn_vs_tp = pd.DataFrame({\n",
    "    \"FN_mean\": fn_cases[X_test.columns].mean(),\n",
    "    \"TP_mean\": tp_cases[X_test.columns].mean()\n",
    "})\n",
    "\n",
    "fn_vs_tp[\"Difference\"] = (\n",
    "    fn_vs_tp[\"FN_mean\"] -\n",
    "    fn_vs_tp[\"TP_mean\"]\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "fn_vs_tp = fn_vs_tp.reindex(\n",
    "    fn_vs_tp[\"Difference\"]\n",
    "    .abs()\n",
    "    .sort_values(ascending=False)\n",
    "    .index\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "fn_vs_tp.head(10)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": [
    "The misclassification analysis shows that the largest differences between false negative and true positive cases appeared in `PhysHlth`, `BMI`, `MentHlth`, `Age`, `Income`, and `GenHlth`.\n",
    "\n",
    "Since the XGBoost model uses the unscaled data, these values can be interpreted in their original units. False negative cases reported fewer days of poor physical health (3.5 vs. 9.1 days) and poor mental health (2.8 vs. 4.8 days), had a lower average BMI (27.7 vs. 32.7), were slightly younger, had a higher income level, and rated their general health as better (2.4 vs. 3.5, where lower values indicate better health). In addition, only 37% of false negative cases had high blood pressure, compared with 85% of true positive cases.\n",
    "\n",
    "This suggests that diabetic cases with less obvious risk patterns are more difficult for the model to identify. This is consistent with the feature importance results, where `HighBP` was the most important feature, meaning that diabetic individuals without high blood pressure are more likely to be missed."
   ],
   "metadata": {
    "id": "L4kuNOUii35s"
   }
